---
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro'
import Callout from 'web-design-system/astro/marketing/Callout.astro'
import CodeBlock from 'web-design-system/astro/product/CodeBlock.astro'
import { Badge } from 'web-design-system/components'
import TableScroll from '../components/TableScroll.astro'

const toc = [
  { label: 'Todoist', href: '#todoist' },
  { label: 'GitHub', href: '#github' },
  { label: 'AI providers', href: '#providers' },
  { label: 'MCP servers, hooks, and proxies', href: '#mcp' },
  { label: 'The agent protocol', href: '#acp' },
]

const providerConfig = `{
  "provider": "auto",
  "model": "auto",
  "providers": {
    "openrouter": {
      "base_url": "https://openrouter.ai/api/v1",
      "api_key": "env:OPENROUTER_API_KEY",
      "models": ["openrouter/free"]
    }
  },
  "combos": {
    "coding": [
      ["poolside", "poolside/laguna-xs-2.1"],
      ["openrouter", "openrouter/free"]
    ]
  },
  "mcp_servers": [
    { "name": "my-tools", "command": "npx", "args": ["-y", "@modelcontextprotocol/server-filesystem", "."] }
  ],
  "hooks": [{ "event": "on_turn_end", "command": "echo turn done" }]
}`

const runs = `cargo run -p agent-cli              # interactive TUI
cargo run -p agent-cli -- --acp      # ACP server over stdio (JSON-RPC 2.0 NDJSON)
cargo run -p agent-cli -- -p "fix the tests"   # single-shot prompt with tools`

const apiKeyForms = [
  { form: '!command', meaning: 'Runs the rest as a shell command and uses its trimmed stdout as the key.' },
  { form: 'env:VAR', meaning: 'Reads the environment variable VAR.' },
  { form: 'anything else', meaning: 'Treated as a literal key.' },
]
---

<Docs
  title="Integrations"
  description="todo-lofi talks to the outside world through opt-in integrations: a task list you already use, a code host, model providers, and the agent protocol."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Callout tone="note" title="Opt-in by design">
    Every integration below is off until you configure credentials for it. The local database stays
    authoritative, and the app keeps working with no network access at all.
  </Callout>

  <h2 id="todoist">Todoist</h2>
  <p>
    Two-way sync with Todoist is the primary external task list. Authentication lives in the app
    (<code>apps/todo-2/src/todoist_auth.rs</code>), the mapping and reconciliation live in
    <code>libs/storage/src/todoist.rs</code>, and the sync pane is
    <code>apps/todo-2/src/ui_parts/todoist_sync.rs</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current spec (<code>docs/spec/todoist-sync-spec.md</code>, draft v1) covers import plus an
    immediate push of title, description, and done deltas; pushing every field is still open. Treat that
    file as the accurate statement of what sync does today.
  </p>

  <h2 id="github">GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub support is in preview and has two halves: issues and projects sync, and a git-backed coding
    workflow.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>apps/todo-2/src/github_auth.rs</code> — authentication.</li>
    <li><code>libs/storage/src/github.rs</code> — sync mappings.</li>
    <li><code>apps/todo-2/src/coding_git.rs</code> — the git side of a coding workflow run.</li>
    <li><code>docs/spec/github-integration-spec.md</code> — the design and its open questions.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Because it is a preview, expect the shape of the mapping to change. <Badge tone="accent" variant="subtle">preview</Badge>
  </p>

  <h2 id="providers">AI providers</h2>
  <p>
    The agent talks to OpenAI-compatible endpoints. Providers are declared in the agent's config, and
    <code>libs/ai_providers</code> supplies the implementations, including an OAuth-backed provider. Every
    field has a built-in default, so you only override what you need.
  </p>
  <p>
    Configuration is layered JSON, merged in this order (later wins): built-in defaults,{' '}
    <code>~/.abstract/config.json</code>, then <code>.abstract/config.json</code> in the project, then
    <code>ABSTRACT_*</code> environment variables, then CLI flags.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename=".abstract/config.json" code={providerConfig} />
  <p>An <code>api_key</code> takes one of three forms:</p>
  <TableScroll label="API key forms">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Form</th>
          <th>Meaning</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          apiKeyForms.map((entry) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{entry.form}</code></td>
              <td>{entry.meaning}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </TableScroll>
  <p>
    A <strong>combo</strong> is a named list of provider/model pairs that appears in the model picker as a
    single choice. Selecting one runs on its first entry and, if the run fails before producing any output
    (a provider error or rate limit), retries on the next. Failures cool down so a rate-limited provider is
    not hammered again, and the cooldowns persist across restarts in a small JSON file.
  </p>
  <p>
    The full provider, combo, and environment-variable reference is in
    <code>apps/agent-cli/README.md</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mcp">MCP servers, hooks, and proxies</h2>
  <p>
    The same config file wires up tool servers and lifecycle hooks. MCP servers are launched as child
    processes and their tools are added to the agent's tool set.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>mcp_servers</code> — each entry names a command and its arguments.</li>
    <li><code>hooks</code> — shell commands bound to agent events such as <code>on_turn_end</code>.</li>
    <li><code>env</code> — environment variables that tools read at runtime, with shell values winning over config values.</li>
    <li><code>proxy</code> — routes provider traffic through a local or remote gateway.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="acp">The agent protocol</h2>
  <p>
    <code>agent-cli</code> doubles as an Agent Client Protocol server, which is how the desktop app's agent
    pane drives it. The protocol runs over stdio as newline-delimited JSON-RPC 2.0 and supports{' '}
    <code>initialize</code>, <code>session/new</code>, <code>session/load</code>,{' '}
    <code>session/prompt</code>, <code>session/set_config_option</code>, and <code>session/cancel</code>,
    with streaming updates as notifications.
  </p>
  <CodeBlock filename="terminal" code={runs} showLineNumbers={false} />
  <p>
    <code>session/new</code> advertises the available providers and models, so a client can switch models
    mid-session; fallback switches are reported to the client too. The desktop side of this is
    <code>libs/acp-client</code> — process launch, sessions, the transcript model, and the file-system and
    terminal capabilities a session needs. See <code>docs/spec/acp-client-and-agent-panel-spec.md</code>.
  </p>
</Docs>
